Now I understand the mechanism completely:

- `SplitModeView` subscribes to `editor.on('change', forceUpdate)` — so on every editor change, it re-invokes `renderPreview({getValue, mode, md, directiveSyntax})`.
- My `renderPreview` returns a React element; each re-invocation creates new props → my preview component re-renders → I should debounce the actual `transform` call and update via effect.

Important subtlety: my preview component must re-render when called with new params. Since `renderPreview` is invoked on each change and returns a new element with a new `getValue` (stable ref) — React re-renders the component because parent re-rendered. Then `useEffect(() => { render() }, [render])` pattern from the guide works if `render` (debounced) changes when `getValue` changes — it doesn't... In the guide, deps are `[props, render]` — `props` is a new object each render → effect fires each render → debounced transform runs. I'll follow the same pattern: run the debounced render on every render of the preview component.

Simpler and robust approach for my preview component:

```tsx
function MarkupPreview({getValue, md}: RenderPreviewParams) {
  const [html, setHtml] = useState(() => renderToHtml(getValue(), md));

  useEffect(() => {
    const update = debounce(() => setHtml(renderToHtml(getValue(), md)), 200);
    // we can't debounce per-render like that...
  });
}
```

Hmm. Let me follow the guide structure precisely, adapted:

```tsx
function MarkupPreview({getValue, md}: RenderPreviewParams) {
  const [html, setHtml] = useState('');

  const render = useMemo(
    () =>
      debounce(() => {
        setHtml(
          transform(getValue(), {
            allowHTML: md.html,
            breaks: md.breaks,
            linkify: md.linkify,
            linkifyTlds: md.linkifyTlds,
            needToSanitizeHtml: true,
          }).result.html,
        );
      }, 200),
    [getValue, md],
  );

  useEffect(() => {
    render(); // called on every render of this component (SplitModeView forceUpdates on change)
  });

  useEffect(() => () => render.cancel(), [render]);

  return <YfmStaticView html={html} className="md-preview" />;
}
```

`useEffect` without deps array runs after every render — good; SplitModeView re-renders us on each 'change'. The debounced function is stable per (getValue, md). Also add cleanup on unmount to cancel pending debounce.

For debounce, write my own:

```ts
function debounce<A extends unknown[]>(fn: (...args: A) => void, ms: number) {
  let t: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
  const debounced = (...args: A) => {
    clearTimeout(t);
    t = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms);
  };
  debounced.cancel = () => clearTimeout(t);
  return debounced;
}
```

`OutputType` — check `.result.html` shape. The guide: `const res = transform(...).result; setHtml(res.html); setMeta(res.meta);` ✓. Let me quickly verify OutputType has `result: {html, meta}`.

Also, do I need `useThemeValue`? The guide had it for theme changes. YfmStaticView's styling adapts via CSS to theme automatically; transform output HTML doesn't depend on theme for basic markdown. I'll skip useThemeValue — fewer moving parts. Actually wait, one more consideration — the transform result for certain plugins (mermaid) depends on theme, but we're not using those plugins. Skip it.

